id: pm-rl-yi7j
title: "Recursive self-improvement: a training loop whose every generation is tracked, gated, and invalidatable"
description: "pm-rl must be able to TRACK AND GATE a loop in which a policy improves itself: a generation collects trajectories, a trainer outside pm-rl produces a successor, and the successor collects the next generation's trajectories. pm-rl never runs the trainer, never schedules compute, and never orchestrates a job; it records what happened, refuses to let a result look valid when its provenance says otherwise, and answers the questions the loop cannot answer about itself. That boundary is deliberate and matches the package's existing no-orchestration scope. The loop is easy to run and almost impossible to trust, because every property that makes a result meaningful degrades silently as it turns. The generating policy is not recorded, so nobody can say which model produced a trajectory. The held-out set quietly becomes training data, so the score rises while capability does not. The proxy objective and the objective the programme actually cares about drift apart, and only the proxy is measured. An environment or reward edit invalidates every downstream generation, and nothing says so. None of these are training problems. They are all provenance problems, which is to say context problems, and pm already models the graph that answers them - but only if the graph carries the right edges. Reachability is not free: a parent edge from a generation to its predecessor does not connect an environment revision to the generations that used it. This epic therefore requires an explicit graph contract as its foundation, not as a later refinement."
type: Epic
status: open
priority: 1
tags: []
created_at: "2026-08-04T20:48:53.609Z"
updated_at: "2026-08-11T10:38:12.114Z"
author: claude
estimated_minutes: 5400
acceptance_criteria: "Every artifact a verdict depends on carries an immutable content-addressed identity: environment version, reward specification version, base checkpoint, candidate tree, collection run, and each recorded score; a generation declares explicit typed edges to its parent generation, its collection runs, and the exact environment and reward-spec versions those runs used, so reverse reachability from an environment edit reaches every downstream generation; promotion refuses when the evaluation set is reachable from the candidate's training data over the declared edge types, and the refusal prints a stable path; the proxy and held-out scores are attributed identically and compared by a defined direction-aware formula; advancing past the approved promotion budget is refused and names the approval item required; pm-rl runs no trainer and registers no command until its acceptance criteria and refusal paths are implemented and tested; 100 percent line, branch, function and docstring coverage"
dependencies[1]{id,kind,created_at,author,source_kind,author_source}:
  pm-rl-e20d,parent,"2026-08-04T20:48:53.609Z",claude,"cli:create:dep",detected
comments[3]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-10T23:05:32.609Z",pi-agent,"Recursive-self-improvement test coverage complete: added test/lineage.test.ts (lineage.ts pure functions plus the generation/lineage command lifecycle through the SDK test harness). All five sub-items (81oc, qix7, zav1, 32a9, gyrj) closed with item-specific evidence. Coverage is now 100/100/100 lines/branches/functions for index.ts and lineage.ts; npm run release:check is fully green; both refusals (contamination, generation budget) were verified revert-proof. Note: isEnvironmentInvalidated was exported (it already carried a docstring, matching index.ts's existing exported-helper pattern) so its empty-environment-id guard could be exercised directly; no threshold was lowered and no ignore/exclusion was added."
  "2026-08-11T06:13:05.975Z","harness:claude-code","Correcting the completion record after a review finding. Five of this epic's six children are closed: pm-rl-81oc generation lineage, pm-rl-qix7 the approved budget, pm-rl-zav1 the proxy-to-held-out gap, pm-rl-32a9 rl lineage, and pm-rl-gyrj the contamination refusal. The ten defect issues opened from the first review round are also all closed.\n\nThe epic stays OPEN on exactly one remaining child: pm-rl-0cqg, the fleet's own mandatory gates as the first sim-to-RL environment. Its criteria are candidate-tree identity, exact replay, pull-request linkage, and paired-cohort reporting. closed_at, completed_at and close_reason must not be set until that child and those criteria are complete.\n\nThis corrects an earlier statement that the remaining roadmap item was the incompatible-version ranking refusal (pm-rl-p401). p401 is real and still open, but it is NOT a child of this epic, so it is not what holds the epic open."
  "2026-08-11T10:38:12.114Z","harness:claude-code","Correcting a count in my earlier completion note. That note said TEN defect issues were opened from the first review round. The repository contains EIGHT: pm-rl-cnc0, pm-rl-k5xd, pm-rl-izu1, pm-rl-qdxu, pm-rl-6fb5, pm-rl-dzqc, pm-rl-o9dr and pm-rl-82qw.\n\nThe confusion was mine and is worth naming precisely, because it is a category error rather than a miscount. The review reported ten FINDINGS, rated one Critical, five Major and four lesser. I tracked those ten findings as eight ITEMS, because several findings shared one root cause and were grouped, for example the three parse and arithmetic checks that became pm-rl-dzqc. Writing ten in a sentence about issues conflated findings with items.\n\nEverything else in that note stands and has been re-verified against the tracked files rather than from memory: five of the six original epic children are closed, pm-rl-0cqg is the single remaining open child, and closed_at, completed_at and close_reason remain unset until its criteria are met.\n\nNote also that a single comment body is stored in both the item projection and the durable history event, so one wrong sentence appears in both places and one corrective entry fixes both."
body: ""
